
## checking the missingness in every sample ##

plink --bfile pca_data \
      --allow-extra-chr \
      --missing \
      --out pca_data


// pca_data.imiss, pca_data.lmiss, pca_data.log, pca_data.nosex - these files get generated.
the .imiss file contains the following details
FID (Family ID), IID (Individual ID) The sample name; MISS_PHENO -Whether the phenotype is missing. (N = phenotype present,Y = phenotype missing)

N_MISS -Number of missing genotypes for that individual.
Example:
N_MISS = 125
means this sample has no genotype call at 125 SNPs.

N_GENO -Total number of genotypes examined.
Example:
N_GENO = 5000
means the individual was evaluated at 5000 SNP loci.

F_MISS -Fraction (proportion) of missing genotypes.
FMISS​= ​N_MISS / N_GENO 

So:
F_MISS = 0.025
means:
2.5% of SNPs are missing (i.e is there is 2.5 % missing data)
97.5% of SNPs have genotype calls
​
## How to interpret F_MISS ##
F_MISS	     Interpretation
< 0.01	       Excellent
0.01–0.05	     Very good
0.05–0.10	     Acceptable
> 0.10	       Often removed
> 0.20	     Usually poor-quality sample​

















